WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.600
Writing code can
improve your workflow.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.770
We, the people in this room
and our friends joining online,

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:18.000
we know this, but
writing code can also

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.440
be tricky, frustrating
and time consuming.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.460
We also know this,
and in my experience,

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.150
it can be difficult to convince
yourself or maybe a manager

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.490
that it is worth
spending time up front

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:35.640
to write code for tasks
like data wrangling,

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.640
especially if there's an
existing process that's already

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.430
good enough.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.970
So my goal today is to
demonstrate that it is worth

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.460
spending this time
upfront to write code,

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:51.870
because in the long run it
will save time, reduce errors

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.240
and improve the workflow.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.520
And I'm going to use some
examples from my work,

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.320
and maybe these
will motivate you

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.390
to go off and write some code.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.150
Or maybe you can
use these examples

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.459
to help convince a
skeptical manager

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:09.180
to let you write that code.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.180
So, Hi everyone.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:11.800
My name is Danielle Dempsey.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.550
I'm a research scientist
at a small company called

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.840
the Center for Marine Applied
Research, or CMAR for short

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.410
in Nova Scotia, Canada.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:26.340
CMAR coordinates this really great
coastal monitoring program

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:29.970
to measure ocean variables like
temperature, dissolved, oxygen

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:34.470
and salinity from all around
the coast of the province.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.050
This data can be
used by scientists

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.980
to study coastal ecosystems,
industry to inform their site

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.340
selection, government to inform
their policy and management

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.280
decisions.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.520
And the list could go on.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.240
So this is really valuable data.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.450
But, for several
years it was rarely

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.660
used because it was just
stored on somebody's desktop

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.390
and a myriad of
CSV and Excel files

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.660
that were really difficult
and time consuming to compile

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.650
into a useful format.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.350
So why was this so difficult?

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:12.770
It has to do with how the
data are collected using

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:15.140
what we call sensor strings.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.570
So a sensor string
consists of a rope

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.760
that's anchored
to the ocean floor

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.600
and suspended just below
the surface by a bouy.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.870
There are sensors attached to
the rope at different depths

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.330
through the water
column, and these sensors

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:34.220
are recording data every
one minute to 1 hour.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.970
Each sensor string is deployed
in one location for a couple

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:39.550
of months, up to a year.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:43.580
So they're really
collecting a lot of data,

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.580
and we use three
different types of sensors

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:50.330
that each report the data back
in a slightly different format

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:53.240
like you can see on the screen.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:54.800
And you can start
to appreciate how

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.310
working with this data can
be challenging, especially

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:01.790
because there can be seven or
more sensors on each sensor

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.220
string in each of
these data files

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:09.470
can be tens of
thousands of rows long.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:13.250
So part of the reason that I
was hired about two years ago

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:17.780
was to help compile all of this
data into a nice, tidy format

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:21.260
that we could post online for
any interested stakeholder

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:25.280
to download and use
in their own analyses.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.940
So about three weeks after
I started this new position,

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:31.250
I had a video call
with my manager

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:34.520
so she could show me how,
show me the existing process

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:38.090
for how this data is compiled.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:40.760
And I can remember
very vividly sitting

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:44.240
in my home office on a
beautiful Thursday afternoon

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:46.640
while she showed
me this process.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.140
And let me tell you
this involved copying.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:52.820
It involved pasting.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:56.150
It involved sorting the
data by a specific column,

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.840
and it even involved writing
down the Excel row numbers

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.780
to use later on in the process.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:07.130
It was truly horrible
and I thought to myself,

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:10.520
I will never do that.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.490
I offered to write some
code to help with this,

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:16.620
but my manager was a little
bit skeptical at first,

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.649
particularly because
at this point

00:04:18.649 --> 00:04:23.060
I wasn't very confident in
my data wrangling or package

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.290
development skills.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.660
But eventually she
gave me the thumbs up

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:31.050
and I wrote a package
called strings

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.710
to help automate a
lot of this process.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.620
To be clear, the package is
named after our sensor strings

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.800
and has nothing to do
with character strings.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.650
So this was maybe a bit
of a naive name choice,

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.750
but it's only caused one
small moment of confusion

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:47.520
so far.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.070
And that's another story.

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.475
For now, I'll move
on to my first point.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:59.080
Writing code will save you time.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:01.810
Yes you'll have to
spend some time up front

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.900
to develop the code and you
may have some learning curves

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.490
to surmount,
particularly if you're

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:08.500
new to R, new to coding.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:12.420
But it will be worth it.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:19.320
So I spent a few weeks
developing the backbone

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.330
of the strings package.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.280
And as I said, at
this time, I didn't

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.970
have a lot of experience
with data wrangling, package

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:30.000
development, working
with dates and times,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.930
or importing and exporting
large data files.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.030
So I spent a pretty
substantial amount

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:38.310
of time looking into
different packages

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:41.310
to help with those challenges.

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:42.990
And these are just
some of the packages

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.590
that I ended up working with.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:47.040
You'll recognize most of them
from the tidyverse verse,

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.940
although there are a couple
from outside of that collection.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:52.860
And the point I
want to make here

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:55.920
is that even though I hadn't
used most of these packages

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.210
before working on strings,
I now use almost all of them

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:02.830
and almost all of
my coding projects.

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:04.440
And that's because
of the time that I

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:08.370
spent learning about them to
work on the strings package.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.200
So this is just
one small example

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.530
of how the skills that you learn
working on one coding project

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:17.790
are often really transferable
to other projects.

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:20.940
And in my case, the time that
I spent working on the strength

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.060
package also saved us
time for processing

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:27.570
that coastal monitoring
program data.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:32.580
We estimate that it would have
taken two years of work hours

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:36.090
to compile all of that data
with the horrendous old copy

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.430
and paste method.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.560
Instead, within a
couple of months

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:43.530
I had developed
the package, myself

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:45.840
and our student at
the time had compiled

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.670
and formatted all of the data
posted online and generated

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:52.355
summary reports
through R Markdown.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:58.520
It's also now really
easy to train new people

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:01.040
to compile this data
using templates that I

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.800
wrote to go with the package.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:09.890
Our current intern has
compiled 71 deployments so far

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:13.430
and on top of all of his other
work in just the 11 weeks

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.980
that he's been with us so far.

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:17.840
That's something
that would have taken

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:24.320
over 9 months of dedicated
work time with the old method,

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:27.260
and on the screen is just
a tiny, tiny snapshot

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:28.580
of our data.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:31.940
So this is temperature
data from six deployments

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:36.980
or about four years from an
area called Birchy Head.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:39.890
And here the temperature was
measured at 7 different depths

00:07:39.890 --> 00:07:43.400
through the water column,
which are shown by color.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:47.240
And if you're interested in
this or any other CMAR data,

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:49.320
feel free to find me
later this afternoon.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:51.270
I'm always really
happy to talk about it.

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:57.630
Writing code to help
with your data wrangling

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:03.120
can also help find and reduce
errors in your data set.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.210
This will give you
and your clients

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:10.170
more confidence in the data
and also make whatever analysis

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:14.830
you plan on doing with
that data more reliable.

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:17.790
And in my experience,
errors can kind of creep

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:20.910
into the data at different
points along the data pipeline,

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:24.240
especially wherever
humans are involved.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.220
And so I've learned
to write code,

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:31.030
to anticipate and sometimes
even fix those, those errors.

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:33.630
I have two examples
of that for you.

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:36.210
For the first one,
after we had finished

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:38.730
compiling all that
sensor string data,

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:42.330
I decided to plot the latitude
and longitude of each sensor

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:47.370
string to get an idea of the
spatial coverage of the data.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:50.400
So you can imagine my
surprise when my map

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:53.280
looks something like this.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:56.280
So most of the sensor
strings are showing up

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:00.150
clustered around Nova Scotia,
which is what we would expect.

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:02.940
But there's also some
sensor strings showing up

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:05.880
way over here in
Kazakhstan, which

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.070
is not close to Nova
Scotia and not even

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:10.650
anywhere near the ocean.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:12.900
So what's going on here?

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:15.720
After a little bit of
digging, I discovered

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:18.870
that our contractors who
actually deploy these sensor

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:22.560
strings have to manually
record the latitude

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.160
and longitude of each location.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:29.310
And because Nova Scotia is
in the Western hemisphere,

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:33.690
longitude is typically
recorded as a negative value.

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:35.940
But every so often
it accidentally

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:38.073
gets entered as a
positive value, which

00:09:38.073 --> 00:09:39.990
is what's causing some
of these sensor strings

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:43.980
to show up way over
there in Central Asia.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:48.180
So now, when the package first
reads in this coordinate data,

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:51.390
I'll get a warning if any
of those longitude values

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:52.490
are positive.

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:54.420
So we can go in and
fix that mistake

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:56.300
before compiling
all of the data.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:02.740
A kind of more insidious
error that I've seen

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:07.750
is data from the wrong
sensor in a file of data

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:10.450
that's ready to be compiled.

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:11.980
And this just
happens when someone

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:15.310
has what I call a human
moment and accidentally puts

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:18.160
the wrong file in the folder.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:21.190
In this example, data
from one of the sensors

00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:23.080
on the blue sensor
string has been

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:25.120
put in the folder
with all of the data

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:28.450
from sensors on the
green sensor string.

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:31.000
And this issue is
really twofold.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.540
One, the data set for
the blue sensor string

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:36.080
will be incomplete.

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:40.090
And two, we will get false
information about the area

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:42.880
where the green sensor
string was deployed.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:44.650
So this is really
a critical error

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:48.090
to be able to find and fix.

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:51.450
Luckily, the data
files are automatically

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:54.660
named with the serial
number of the sensor

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:56.910
and we keep track
of which sensors

00:10:56.910 --> 00:11:01.380
are associated with each sensor
string in deployment logs.

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.870
And so I've written
code in the package that

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:07.050
will give an error if
there's any missing

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:10.230
extra or different
files in that folder

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:12.185
based on what we would
expect from the log.

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:21.290
Writing code to help automate
your data wrangling can also

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:24.638
make your workflow more
traceable and reproducible,

00:11:24.638 --> 00:11:26.180
especially when
compared to something

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:28.250
like copying and pasting.

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:31.340
And you can explain this
to your non-coding managers

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:34.400
with a metaphor.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:36.980
So copying and
pasting in Excel is

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:38.750
kind of like making
a salad from just

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:41.240
whatever is left in your fridge.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:45.230
Most of the time it
will probably taste OK,

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:47.390
but eventually you
will get something

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:49.940
that doesn't taste quite right.

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:53.450
And because you have no record
of what went into that salad,

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:55.370
it's really hard to
tell what exactly

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:58.910
is making it taste
funny, and it's also

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:03.210
hard to avoid making the
same mistake in the future.

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:05.760
As my colleague said when I
was trying this metaphor out

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.430
on her, you can't take
the dressing back out

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:09.050
of the saddle.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:16.200
In contrast, writing code is
more like writing and following

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:17.520
a recipe.

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:20.190
You can follow it step by
step to get a delicious

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:22.710
salad every time.

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:25.500
And as an added bonus, you
can even give this recipe

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.590
to a friend so they can
make their own salad

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:30.470
or help you make dinner.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:37.770
As a more concrete
example for another part

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:39.600
of our coastal
monitoring program,

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:42.630
we had these 65
current data sets

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:46.410
that had to be compiled from
a kind of messy text format

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:51.570
into a tidier CSV format
that was easier to work with.

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:54.420
So I offered to write some
code to help with this,

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.480
but my managers again decided
to have a couple of people

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:02.820
instead copy, paste
and format in Excel.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:07.440
This took hours and hours
of several people's time,

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:10.560
but eventually they finished.

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:13.500
And then we
discovered that there

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:16.650
had been a small mistake
in how these original text

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:19.770
files were exported from
the sensor software.

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:24.940
So all of the data needed
to be compiled again.

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:29.900
So this time I was asked
to write some code.

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:32.060
And within a few
weeks I had written

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:35.810
a package that automated
most of this workflow.

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:40.040
So now if we want to make any
other changes to this process,

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:41.870
we've actually made a couple.

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:45.230
I just need to go in, tweak
the code, press a button,

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:47.137
and all of this data
will be compiled

00:13:47.137 --> 00:13:48.220
within just a few minutes.

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:55.190
And so if you have a
repetitive task that you've

00:13:55.190 --> 00:13:57.080
been thinking
about automating, I

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:59.120
encourage you to go home
after the conference

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:01.490
and start writing some code.

00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:04.200
You don't have to write
a whole package in a day.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:05.960
You can start with
a function or two

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:08.720
and just see where
it goes from there.

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:12.020
And if you do get some pushback
from a skeptical manager,

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:16.700
remind them that writing
code will save time,

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:20.030
reduce errors, and
improve the workflow.

00:14:20.030 --> 00:14:23.450
And if you do use those points
and it works, please let me

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:26.300
know, because it
would make my day.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:31.100
And the last thing I'll say is
that my previously skeptical

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:34.610
manager is now totally on
board and really supportive

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:36.170
of all my coding.

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:40.070
She even sewed me this
